<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03CL">Remark <span data-tag="03CL">7.5.6.16</span> <span class="named">(The Homotopy Colimit as a Left Derived Functor)</span>.</a> The preceding results can be interpreted in the language of model categories. For every small category $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, the category $\operatorname{Fun}( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }})$ can be equipped with a model structure in which the fibrations are levelwise Kan fibrations and weak equivalences are levelwise weak homotopy equivalences (see Example <a class="" data-content="This is a reference to something which is not yet available in Kerodon." data-html="true" data-placement="bottom" data-toggle="popover" data-trigger="focus" role="button" style="cursor: pointer;" tabindex="0" title="Future reference"><img src="/static/images/question.svg"/></a>). Combining Propositions <a data-tag="03CD" href="/tag/03CD">7.5.6.9</a> and <a data-tag="03CG" href="/tag/03CG">7.5.6.12</a>, we deduce that the homotopy colimit functor $ \underset { \longrightarrow }{\mathrm{holim}}: \operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ can be viewed as a <em>left derived</em> functor of the usual colimit $\varinjlim : \operatorname{Fun}(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}, \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}) \rightarrow \operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ (see Definition <a class="" data-content="This is a reference to something which is not yet available in Kerodon." data-html="true" data-placement="bottom" data-toggle="popover" data-trigger="focus" role="button" style="cursor: pointer;" tabindex="0" title="Future reference"><img src="/static/images/question.svg"/></a>). </p>
</article>
